From b6616141c7da2fa1fd1ae1027728a1bfe0ce88a1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: arealjim <6187715+arealjim@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 08:09:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] runtime: review close-out 2026-10-21 (H11 H12 M14-M18 M20 M21
 L12) - rate lock off by default and capability-gated, per-session pacing
 state, sane latency reports, USB admission, RTT probe only when read, pacer
 flushes on stop

Closes the Mac runtime findings of the 2026-10-21 adversarial review that
were still open. Nothing here changes what a worn stream does with the
default config except where a finding said it should.

H11 / M14: pacing_lock_wait_ms defaults to 0 (off). When set, the rate lock
also requires the client to advertise CLIENT_CAPABILITY_PACING_LOCK
(0x00002000, new in Protocol.h; 0x800 is FOVEATION_CENTER on the Frame client branch): the trim is driven by numbers the client
reports, so a client whose LatencyReport means something else must not
steer the app's cadence. LatencyReports with a non-finite or out-of-range
field are dropped whole before they reach any atomic (LatencyReportCheck.h,
unit-tested), and WaitFrame checks the error and the trim for finiteness
before the cast - NaN in the PI integral was a NaN trim forever and
(long long)NaN is undefined.

M15: the pacing diagnostic accumulators and the rate lock's PI state move
from function statics into Session members, so they die with the session
and the client they were tuned against. The pacing_thread policy is applied
per thread (thread_local), the time-constraint computation is a third of
the period and the constraint half of it (2 ms / 5 ms before), and the
mach_thread_self send right is released.

M16: Config::GetSnapshot() returns a shared_ptr to the current values,
replaced whole on reload. WaitFrame reads the config once per frame through
it (three copies per frame before), and the encode thread's per-frame copy
is gone too.

H12: video_send_port (default 9944, 0 = ephemeral) and the bind is made
without SO_REUSEADDR: a port another process holds - the desktop simulator
binds 9944 on the same Mac - now fails the bind and falls back to an
ephemeral port with a warning, instead of sharing the port silently. The
default stays fixed because the Steam Frame's tailnet firewall zone only
passes video the client pre-opened (2026-10-13).

M17: the USB connect path runs the same admission decision as WiFi (its
identity is 127.0.0.1 plus the device name), resets the tracking history,
the sample timers and the RTT target, and reads the capabilities.

M18: admission compares the device name as well as the address where both
sides report one, so two devices behind one NAT, or a simulator on the
host of a USB client, are told apart. The protocol has no refusal message;
the refusal is logged (rate-limited) and the client keeps retrying.

M20: rtt_probe = "auto" | "on" | "off" (auto = only while pose_prediction is
"measured", the one mode that reads it; the probe sent ICMP/UDP from inside
every app before). rtt_assumed_ms replaces the compiled-in 5.3. The UDP
fallback no longer latches for the life of the process: a new target gets
ICMP tried afresh.

M21: the video send thread is joined before its socket is closed, and the
pacer flushes when running_ goes false, so a paced frame can no longer send
through a descriptor closed under it.

L12: the prediction target timestamp is a saturating add of a bounded
finite delta (the timestamp is the client's); the located-pose ring grows
16 -> 64; the pacer's datagram count and the sender share one function
(DatagramsForNal, unit-tested against the sender's packetisation).

M19 is not changed: StreamingServer::EncodeThread already forces a
keyframe every keyframe_interval_sec whatever the VideoToolbox session
kind, so the periodic IDR the finding asked for exists; the Config.h and
toml comments that said otherwise are corrected.

Tests: 108 cases / 1076 assertions in oxrsys_runtime_tests, all passing;
ctest 6/6 in build/macos-arm64-metalfix.
